# Patches applied on every platform, before series.<platform>. The libass pin
# is shared by all four groups. This series is what used to be the
# edde746/libass fork, re-rooted on upstream: the fork's history had no
# common ancestor with libass/libass, so a pin bump was a tree diff either
# way, and a fork-level fix had to wait for a fork release before it could
# ship. Keep the split: a bump that conflicts tells you which feature it
# conflicts with. In order:
#   fast blur          ASS_FEATURE_FAST_BLUR: kernels sized to the blur radius
#   parse/rasterize    parser and rasterizer speedups, no API change
#   threads            thread-safe caches, per-event threaded rendering
#                      (ass_set_threads), the image pool, the static-event
#                      layout cache, and the build-system threading option;
#                      the fork's font-guard and layout-cache fixes are
#                      folded in
#   shape cache        HarfBuzz results cached per run (font, size, segment
#                      properties, features, text and its context), so
#                      animated and frame-by-frame typesetting stops
#                      re-shaping the same text every frame
#   fonts dir          fonts_dir loaded once per library, mapped read-only
#                      on POSIX, instead of copied again by every renderer
#   frame refs         ass_frame_ref/ass_frame_unref exported, so a caller
#                      can hold an image list (and its bitmaps) across renders
# The rest renders the same bytes faster (compared frame by frame, on every
# architecture, over a corpus of typeset episodes and libass' own tests):
#   key padding        cache keys hashed member by member where the ABI pads
#                      them (ARM EABI): the threads patch hashed the padding,
#                      so ARMv7 missed its caches and re-rendered at random
#   event blocks       a frame walks the blocks of 64 events whose time span
#                      holds it, not every event of the track
#   blur scratch       stripe buffers and the blur's input kept per thread,
#                      a single cached bitmap blurred without a copy
#   shadow shift       ass_shift_bitmap as passes the compiler vectorizes
#   thread wakeups     workers woken only for events that need rendering;
#                      the default thread count is the cores near the
#                      fastest (cpu_capacity), not every core
#   drawings           drawing tokens in one array, plain decimals parsed in
#                      one pass, one tag walk per event text
0001-ass-fast-blur-kernels.patch
0002-ass-parse-and-rasterize-faster.patch
0003-ass-threaded-rendering-image-pool-and-layout-cache.patch
0004-ass-cache-shaping-results.patch
0005-ass-load-the-fonts-directory-once-per-library.patch
0006-ass-export-frame-references.patch
0007-ass-never-hash-the-padding-of-a-cache-key.patch
0008-ass-skip-the-events-that-cannot-be-active.patch
0009-ass-blur-in-scratch-memory.patch
0010-ass-shift-shadows-a-row-at-a-time.patch
0011-ass-wake-workers-only-for-events-to-render.patch
0012-ass-parse-drawings-and-numbers-faster.patch
